  {
   "id": "d6tmoZAKDgAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2017-02-06T10:12:40Z",
   "subject": "Re: Long and wide tables, and lavaan",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Will lavaan understand this data orientation?<br></div></blockquote><div> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Whatever your sampling unit of interest is (e.g., occasions, individuals), lavaan expects data in wide format (one row per sampling unit).  It does not (yet) have features for analyzing multilevel data.  The lavaan.survey package can adjust standard errors for clustering, but I&#39;m not sure whether that is relevant for you.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Or should I reduce the dimensionality of my dataset and perform a principal component analysis for each of the test domains?</div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I&#39;m not sure what that would accomplish.  A structural equation model is meant to represent a theoretical causal model, so the entities in the model should be real interpretable variables.  But this is beyond the scope of a software question.  SEMNET is available for general SEM questions, and hopefully someone in that wide audience could provide guidance on appropriate ways to use SEM for neuroimaging data.</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792427576787000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2r5UOcTtguLnigIR7mKSEJ\">www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html</a><br></div><div><br></div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div>UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792427576787000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3SQt1DSaIShVXuUBu2d1HU\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<div title=\"MDH:SGkgZXZlcnlvbmUsPGJyPjxicj5JIGFtIHJlbGF0aXZlbHkgbmV3IHRvIGxhdmFhbiwgdGh1cyBJ\r\nIGFtIG5vdCB1c2VkIHRvIHNvbWUgc3RhdGlzdGljYWwgdGVybXMuIFNvIGJlIHBhdGllbnQuIFRo\r\naXMgaXMgbXkgZmlyc3QgdGltZSBwb3N0aW5nIGhlcmU6IGZpcnN0IG9mIGFsbCwgdGhhbmtzIHRo\r\nZSBkZXZlbG9wcGVycyBmb3IgdGhlIGV4Y2VsbGVudCB0b29sLjxicj48YnI+PGJyPkkgYW0gZGVh\r\nbGluZyB3aXRoIGEgbGFyZ2UgZGF0YXNldCB0aGF0IEkgaW50ZW5kIHRvIHVzZSBpbiBhIFNFTS4g\r\nVGhlIG1vZGVsIGRlc2lnbiBJIGhhdmUgaW4gbWluZCBpcyBhIG1lZGlhdGlvbi